item.description:
A six-digit secret that authorises payments. Three endpoints, and the mental model is: GET status → VERIFY when required → ACTION.

─── Enable ───
  GET status (needs `canEnable`)
  → POST /auth/2fa {"action":"credential-otp"}
  → POST /settings/security/payment-pin {"action":"enable"}

─── Update / Disable ───
  GET status → pick one of `update.methods`
  → POST /auth/2fa {"action":"credential-otp"}
  → POST /settings/security/payment-pin/verify   (stores `paymentPinChallengeId`)
  → POST /settings/security/payment-pin {"action":"update"|"disable"}

The challenge is single-use and bound to your account, your login, and the ONE operation it was verified for: a challenge verified for `update` cannot disable the PIN, and vice versa. It expires after five minutes.

For `method: "passkey"`, fetch assertion options first from POST /auth/2fa {"action":"reauth-challenge"} — inherent to WebAuthn, not a policy step.
